[[Image:Etruscan_amphora_Louvre_E703_side_B.jpg|thumb|An Etruscan amphora]]

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|amphora}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἀμφορεύς||vase-shaped ornament with a narrow neck}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈæm.fə.ɹə/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|amphorae|s}}

# A two-[[handle]]d [[jar]] with a [[narrow]] [[neck]] that was used in [[ancient]] times to store or carry [[wine]] or [[oil]].
# One of various units for measuring liquid or volume during the Roman Empire, measuring between 18.5 and 39 [[litre]]s depending on the variant.
# An [[at sign]] (@).

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|amphoric}}
* {{sense|unit}} {{l|en|amphora quadrantal}}, {{l|en|Roman amphora}}, {{l|en|Greek amphora}}, {{l|en|French amphora}},

